WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:03.837
"Chewie, wait, wait, don't!"

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.541
You've been caught!

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:11.144
With your pants on the ground!

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:13.480
And you're out of toilet paper.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.915
And you can't get anymore.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:16.950
At least, not how

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.185
you'd normally go about getting it.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:23.456
But toilet paper isn't the only thing you need.

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.892
There are five food groups.

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:27.360
But all you've got

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.196
are empty cupboards.

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:45.678
They range in size, and style,

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.514
and the type of goods they provide.

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:50.216
But, these are the places where most people

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:52.685
in developed countries get their food.

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:54.087
And they often get

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:56.589
much more than they need.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:58.558
Grocery stores provide necessities,

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:01.761
but they're also designed to make money.

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:03.463
There is a science to the way that

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:05.532
grocery stores are laid out.

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:07.600
They entice customers to purchase

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.503
items they don't necessarily need.

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.972
And this explains why some grocery stores

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:16.543
stock as many as 45,000 different items,

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:18.878
while the average consumer only needs

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.881
about 300 items in their home.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.105
For those tech-savvy,

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:47.340
computer wizards among you viewers,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.409
yes, we know, you can do

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:51.744
all your shopping online.

00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:58.551
But a world without grocery retailers

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:01.721
would have fewer grocery wholesalers.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:03.423
The food you'd want would still exist,

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:05.625
but it wouldn't be as readily available,

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.894
because massive supply chains

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:09.596
will have been disrupted.

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:11.598
And even if you were willing to pay

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:14.100
a higher price for online groceries,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.702
some of the items on your list,

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:17.470
like fresh produce,

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.372
would be harder to get.

00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:20.907
Maybe you like to start your day

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:22.809
with a healthy dose of potassium,

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.110
or something sweet,

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.979
like a chocolate croissant.

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:28.448
Well, if you live in the U.S. or Canada,

00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:29.716
for example,

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:32.185
you'd have to change your routine.

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:35.488
Bananas and cacao aren't grown there.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:36.656
And with fewer

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:39.292
international exports and imports, 

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:40.860
the goods we love from abroad

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.429
would be harder to obtain.

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:44.597
From now on,

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:46.266
most of the flavors you'd taste

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:49.135
would be much more domestic.

00:02:56.276 --> 00:02:58.344
So while we wouldn't enjoy the convenience

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.547
or the variety we take for granted today,

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:03.650
we wouldn't be in a total crisis.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.818
In fact, we'd probably live

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.420
healthier, happier lives.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.089
And we'd save some money too!

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.525
For the items you couldn't get online,

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.294
you'd have to visit specialty stores

00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:15.662
or local farms

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:16.896
to get your produce,

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:17.664
meat,

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:18.298
fish,

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:18.831
wheat,

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.400
and dairy.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.069
While it would be incredibly time-consuming,

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.772
it would make you a smarter consumer.

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:27.740
You'd think more about what you need,

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.943
and wouldn't be as easily exposed

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:31.077
to impulse marketing,

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:33.613
like all that candy at the register.

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.448
You could also just start growing

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:37.684
your own fruits and vegetables.

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:39.018
Neighborhoods could establish

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.386
community gardens,

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.255
which have been proven to reduce crime

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:44.357
and would provide low-income families

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:46.659
with access to healthy food.

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.561
Community gardens also improve

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:50.930
local air and soil quality.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.665
They increase biodiversity,

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:54.567
and they reduce neighborhood waste

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.769
through composting.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.838
But if we're okay for food,

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:02.408
what about toilet paper and other necessities?

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:03.977
Okay, so toilet paper

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:05.178
you might have to buy online.

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:06.779
Or you could just take a shower

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.715
when you're done your business.

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:12.185
Or you could treat yourself and buy a bidet.

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:15.088
Who knows? It could be fun!

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:17.957
As for soap, you can make that yourself too.

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:18.958
All you really need is

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:23.663
So while we've proven that

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:24.731
we could survive,

00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:28.001
and probably thrive without grocery stores,

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:29.202
making the change would

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:31.371
definitely be a hard adjustment.

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:32.505
We could probably build

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.140
happier and healthier communities

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.375
if grocery stores closed,

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:39.245
but we'd also have to adapt our tastes.

00:04:39.245 --> 00:04:42.148
There are pros and cons to grocery stores.

00:04:42.148 --> 00:04:44.217
One of the benefits is being able to taste

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:46.219
flavors from faraway places,

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:47.387
which we wouldn't be able

00:04:47.387 --> 00:04:50.323
to access so easily without grocery stores.

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:52.558
Then again, grocery stores encourage

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:54.427
people to buy beyond their needs,

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:57.230
and spend beyond their means.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:01.000
And the food that isn't sold goes to waste.

00:05:07.507 --> 00:05:08.841
Now, while we might not see

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:11.844
a mass closure of grocery stores anytime soon

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.614
we can still learn from this WHAT IF

00:05:14.614 --> 00:05:17.183
by thinking more about our shopping habits,

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:20.119
and becoming smarter consumers.

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:22.588
Grocery stores are a good reminder that

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:24.891
we all have the same needs.

00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:27.694
So think harder about what you really need,

00:05:27.694 --> 00:05:31.464
while considering other peoples' needs as well.

00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:34.233
But if you think we're all big spenders now, 

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:35.835
can you imagine how much more

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:40.940
we'd consume if humans were 5 m (16 ft) tall?

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:46.479
Well, that's a story for another WHAT IF.

